<https://w3id.org/mailo#hasObligation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <https://w3id.org/mailo#ComplianceObligation> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#PLD_Art9> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Courts may order defendants to disclose evidence about high-risk AI systems; creates procedural discovery mechanism absent in AILD."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#C_307_22> <https://w3id.org/mailo#hasOutcome> "First Chamber, 26 October 2023 (FT v DW): a dentist refused a patient a free first copy of his medical records, relying on German law requiring patients to bear the cost. The Court held that Arts.12(5) and 15(1) and (3) GDPR oblige the controller to supply a first copy free of charge irrespective of the requester's reasons, that Art.23(1)(i) does not permit national law to shift that cost to protect the controller's economic interests, and that in a doctor-patient relationship the copy must be a faithful and intelligible reproduction, extending to full documents where necessary to understand the data." .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#GXDBio> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/mailo#NPE> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#proposedRenumberingTo> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#Amend_AIAct_v3a_Omnibus_AnnexIII_Standalone> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Activation event: high_risk_annex_iii_omnibus" .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#C_817_19> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/identifier> "ECLI:EU:C:2022:491" .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#AIAct_v4_FRIA_PublicBodies> <https://w3id.org/mailo#versionStartDate> "2027-08-02"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#EUDirective> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/mailo#LegalInstrument> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#GDPR_Art35> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/mailo#LegalArticle> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#PLD_Art7> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A product is defective where it does not provide the safety that a person is entitled to expect or that is required under Union or national law. The assessment takes account of all circumstances, including presentation and instructions, reasonably foreseeable use and misuse, the effect of the ability to continue to learn or of software updates after placing on the market, the moment in time at which the product was placed on the market or remained within the manufacturer's control, and relevant product safety requirements including cybersecurity.\n\nCORRECTED in v5.1.0: previously labelled 'AI and Software as Products' and describing the product definition, which is Art.4. The continued-learning limb recorded here is the provision on which MAILO's model-drift arguments actually rest."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#TSD_Art3> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Acquisition is lawful where obtained by independent discovery or creation, or by observation, study, disassembly or testing of a product or object that has been made available to the public or is lawfully in the possession of the acquirer who is free of any legally valid duty to limit the acquisition — that is, REVERSE ENGINEERING is lawful acquisition unless contractually excluded. Directly relevant to medical AI: analysing a deployed model's behaviour through observation and testing is not an unlawful acquisition of the trade secrets it embodies, which bears on both explainability research and the practical reach of secrecy over deployed systems. Added in v5.1.0."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#lit_Pandit_H_J_et_al_2024_Data_Privacy_Vocabulary_DPV> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/date> "2024" .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#identifiesInputFactors> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "identifies input factors"@en .
