<https://w3id.org/mailo#TSD_Art5> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "An application for the measures, procedures and remedies is dismissed where the acquisition, use or disclosure was carried out (a) for exercising the right to freedom of expression and information as set out in the Charter, including respect for the freedom and pluralism of the media; (b) for revealing misconduct, wrongdoing or illegal activity in order to protect the general public interest; (c) by workers to their representatives in the legitimate exercise of representative functions; or (d) for the purpose of protecting a legitimate interest recognised by Union or national law.\n\nCORRECTED in v5.1.0, and this was a substantive error rather than a citation slip. The earlier entry read that trade secret protection does not apply 'where disclosure is required for the exercise of the right to information', and treated Art.5 as the provision engaged when a data subject invokes GDPR Art.15(1)(h). Art.5(a) is the freedom of expression and information of Charter Art.11 — media freedom, investigative journalism, protection of journalistic sources per recital 19. A patient seeking an explanation of an automated medical decision is not exercising that right. Art.5(d) might be argued to cover data-protection rights as a legitimate interest recognised by Union law, but that is an argument to be made rather than a plain reading, and the CJEU did not take that route in C-203/22: the Court resolved the trade-secret defence within the GDPR itself, requiring disclosure to the supervisory authority or court for balancing. The provision that actually preserves regulatory disclosure duties is Art.1(2)(b), now encoded separately."@en .
