<https://w3id.org/mailo#MDR_AnnexVIII_Rule11> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Software intended to provide information used to take decisions with diagnosis or therapeutic purposes is Class IIa, rising to Class III where such decisions may cause death or an irreversible deterioration of health, and to Class IIb where they may cause a serious deterioration or surgical intervention. Software intended to monitor physiological processes is Class IIa, or IIb where monitoring vital parameters whose variation could result in immediate danger. All other software is Class I.\n\nThis is the linchpin connecting MDR to the AI Act and was absent from MAILO. Rule 11 places most diagnostic and decision-support software at Class IIa or above, which requires notified-body conformity assessment, which in turn makes the system high-risk under AI Act Art.6(1) by the ANNEX I route — and therefore outside the Art.86 right to explanation. The dual-track burden and the Art.86 exclusion both follow from this classification rule. Added in v5.1.0."@en .
