<https://w3id.org/mailo#Tension_DataAct_TradeSecret_AITransparency> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Re-anchored in v5.1.0 to TSD Art.1(2)(b), which preserves rules requiring disclosure of trade secrets to administrative or judicial authorities. The Art.5 exceptions previously relied on concern freedom of expression and whistleblowing, not data-subject or regulatory disclosure."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#Tension_DataAct_TradeSecret_AITransparency> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Article links added in v5.1.0: DataAct Art.4(6)-(8) and Art.5(9)-(11) for the trade-secret limb, AI Act Art.13 and GDPR Art.15(1)(h) for the transparency limb. Note the asymmetry now visible in the graph: Art.13's addressee is the deployer, not the affected person, and Art.86's right to explanation does not reach medical devices on the Annex I route, so on the transparency side only GDPR Art.15(1)(h) actually runs to the individual."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#C_307_22> <https://w3id.org/mailo#decidedBy> <https://w3id.org/mailo#CJEU_FirstChamber> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#PLD_Art5> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Member States shall ensure that any natural person who suffers damage caused by a defective product is entitled to compensation. The strict-liability rule itself: fault need not be shown, only damage, defectiveness and the causal link. Added in v5.1.0, the strict-liability content having previously been attributed to Art.4."@en .
