<https://w3id.org/mailo#hasVersion> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Regulation has a temporal applicability version" .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#decidedBy> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> <https://w3id.org/mailo#Court> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#hasPredeterminedCriteria> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://w3id.org/mailo#AutomatedDecisionSystem> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#HighRiskRoute> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The route by which an AI system becomes high-risk under the AI Act. Encoded in v5.1.0 because the distinction determines whether Art.86 and Art.26(11) apply at all, and MAILO had recorded it nowhere."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#C_817_19> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/mailo#CJEUCase> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#Tension_DataAct_TradeSecret_AITransparency> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "C-203/22 Dun & Bradstreet §§59-62 limits the trade-secret defence: a controller cannot refuse all explanation on trade-secret grounds, and must describe the procedure and principles actually applied so the data subject understands which personal data were used and in what way. The precise boundary between permitted limitation and impermissible derogation is determined case by case, before a supervisory authority or court. NOTE: the Data Act limb of this tension was previously anchored to Art.15; the trade-secret safeguards are at Arts.4(6)-(8) and 5(9)-(11)."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#ProceduralPrecedent> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The case is relevant for procedure or forum practice rather than substance. Carries no implication about the technology at issue."@en .
