<https://w3id.org/mailo> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "\r\n        Ontology capturing the legal landscape governing AI-driven automated\r\n        decision-making in medical contexts. Covers CJEU precedents on GDPR\r\n        Art.22, UPC/EPO patent litigation in MedTech, the EU AI Act high-risk\r\n        regime, MDR 2017/745, PLD 2024/2853, EHDS 2025/327, DGA 2022/868,\r\n        and Data Act 2023/2854 — with full precedent chains, SHACL runtime\r\n        constraints, and compliance obligation mappings.\r\n        Imports: W3C DPV v2.0 (data privacy vocabulary), AIRO (AI risk ontology),\r\n        ELI (European Legislation Identifier), ECLI (European Case Law Identifier).\r\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#C_307_22> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "SCOPE CORRECTION (v5.1.0): this case involved NO artificial intelligence. It concerned dental records and the cost of a subject access request. The instance previously asserted involvesTechnology MedicalAISystem, an interpretesArticle link to GDPR Art.15(1)(h) (the logic of automated decision-making, not at issue here), and an outcome mentioning AI-generated diagnostic data. All three are removed. Note that this error survived the Part V technology constraint because the case was declared DirectSubjectMatter: that shape tests whether a medical AI claim is consistent with the declared basis, not whether the declared basis is itself correct."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#hasDPIA> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Links a medical AI system to its DPIA record. Required for all high-risk automated processing (GDPR Art.35; C-817/19 P_817_005)."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/modified> "2026-08-12"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#hasPlaintiff> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Plaintiff in the case" .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#RatioDecidendi> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The binding legal reasoning upon which the decision rests."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#PLD_Art10> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Art.10 carries BOTH presumptions. Art.10(2): defectiveness is presumed where the defendant fails to disclose evidence, the product does not comply with mandatory safety requirements, or an obvious malfunction occurred. Art.10(4): defectiveness and/or the causal link are presumed where the claimant faces excessive difficulty owing to technical or scientific complexity and has demonstrated that the defect or the link is probable — the limb of most consequence for medical AI, expressly contemplated at recital 48. Art.10(5) preserves the defendant's right to rebut."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#conflictsWith> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Two legal obligations or principles that are in tension."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#DataSubjectRight> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Data Subject Right"@en .
