<https://w3id.org/mailo#Charter_Art8> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "EU Charter Art.8 — Protection of personal data"@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#Tension_PLD_AIAct_Liability> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "\r\n        PLD Art.4 imposes strict liability; AI Act conformity assessment is not a\r\n        defence to PLD claims. A CE-marked AI-SaMD that passes AI Act conformity\r\n        assessment may still be 'defective' under PLD if it fails in real-world\r\n        deployment. The two regimes do not create a safety harbour from each other.\r\n    "@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#DataAct> <https://w3id.org/mailo#coversArticle> <https://w3id.org/mailo#DataAct_Art4> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#lit_Golpayegani_D_Pandit_H_J_Lewis_D_2022_2025_AIRO> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/mailo#LiteratureSource> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#riskLevel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://w3id.org/mailo#ComplianceRisk> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#Tension_EHDS_GDPR_OptOut> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "EHDS Art.71 grants natural persons a right to opt out of secondary use of their personal electronic health data, enforced by GDPR supervisory authorities. (Renumbered in v5.1.0 from Art.35, which was COM(2022) 197 proposal numbering. The earlier description of the right as 'unconditional' is not verified against the final text and has been removed.) Where opt-out rates are elevated in a clinically significant subpopulation, the resulting training dataset violates AI Act Art.10(3) representativeness. No legislative safe harbour bridges these positions."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#Risk_EPOUPC_Divergence> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "EPO-UPC Divergent Validity Rulings (Opt-Out Strategy)"@en .
