<https://w3id.org/mailo#hasRisk> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://w3id.org/mailo#Technology> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#DataAct_Art21> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A public sector body, the Commission, the ECB or a Union body may share data received under Chapter V with individuals or organisations conducting scientific research or analytics compatible with the purpose of the request, or with national statistical institutes and Eurostat. Recipients assume the obligations in Art.17(3) and Art.19, and organisations under significant commercial influence likely to result in preferential access to results are excluded. Relevant to medical-AI research using data obtained through the exceptional-need route. Added in v5.1.0."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#isActionable> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#EHDS_Art73> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Secondary use of electronic health data must take place within a secure processing environment under the control of a health data access body, from which raw data cannot be downloaded. This is the medical-domain reconciliation route for the trade-secret / transparency tension: disclosure to a controlled environment rather than to the requester. Implementing acts under Art.73(5) apply from 26 March 2029. Added in v5.1.0."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#C_307_22> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/identifier> "C-307/22" .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#AIAct_Art13> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Art.13(1): high-risk AI systems shall be designed and developed so that their operation is sufficiently transparent to enable DEPLOYERS to interpret the output and use it appropriately. Art.13(2)-(3): instructions for use must accompany the system, specifying provider identity, characteristics, capabilities and limitations of performance including accuracy, robustness and cybersecurity metrics, known foreseeable risks, human oversight measures and expected lifetime.\n\nSCOPE NOTE added in v5.1.0: the addressee is the DEPLOYER, not the affected person. Art.13 confers nothing on a patient or data subject. This is the pivot of the trade-secret debate — a provider may satisfy Art.13 in full while an affected individual receives nothing, which is why the explanation route for individuals runs through GDPR Art.15(1)(h) and Art.22, and, in the health domain, through EHDS Art.52's controlled-access mechanism. The earlier one-line comment did not record the addressee distinction on which that argument depends."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#EUDirective> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "EU Directive"@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#hasPrecedenceLevel> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "binding_ratio | persuasive_obiter | constitutional_grand_chamber | authorial_transposition. The first three record how much interpretive weight a holding claims. The fourth marks a mapping the ontology designer has drawn across instruments or contexts, which is NOT a holding of the Court and must not be cited as one. This is an analytical metadata model adopted by MAILO, not a formal precedent hierarchy operated by the CJEU."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#Charter_Art7> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "EU Charter Art.7 — Respect for private and family life"@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#DPIARecord> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A Data Protection Impact Assessment document required for high-risk automated processing (GDPR Art.35; C-817/19 P_817_005)."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#hasStatus> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#Patent> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Patent"@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#LegalInstrument> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Legal Instrument"@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#DataAct_Art3> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Connected products shall be designed and manufactured, and connected services designed and provided, so that product data and related service data are, by default, directly accessible to the user in a simple, secure, free-of-charge, comprehensive, structured, commonly used and machine-readable format. Art.3(2)-(3) add pre-contractual information duties covering the nature, format and estimated volume of the data, whether it is generated in real time, and where it is stored. Applies to products placed on the market from 12 September 2026.\n\nCORRECTED in v5.1.0: the earlier entry described Art.3 as an 'IoT Data Access Right ... enforceable against manufacturers'. That is Art.4. Art.3 is a design and by-default-accessibility obligation; the user's right to obtain data from the data holder is separate and has applied since 12 September 2025. For connected medical devices the distinction is practical, not merely formal: a device placed on the market before September 2026 is outside the design duty while its holder is already subject to the access duty."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#GDPR_Art15> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Right of access by the data subject."@en .
