<https://w3id.org/mailo#lit_Medical_Law_Review_2024_A_health-conformant_reading_of_t> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "Only article addressing the exact intersection of GDPR Art.22 + medical AI. Doctrinal foundation for MAILO's medical-AI ADM rules." .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#triggersObligation> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Principle triggers a compliance obligation" .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#PLD_Art6> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/mailo#LegalArticle> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#Tension_TSD_AIAct_Transparency> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "TSD Art.2 protects model weights, training data schemas, and architecture as trade secrets. AI Act Art.13 requires deployers to receive sufficient information about high-risk AI system capabilities and limitations. C-203/22 §§59-62 sets the mandatory floor: controller cannot invoke TSD Art.2 to deny all causal explanation of an automated decision — disputed information must be produced to the competent supervisory authority or court for balance. AI Act Art.78 ensures national authority confidentiality but does not disapply Art.13 disclosure to deployers. TSD Art.5 is the only legislative bridge but its scope in the medical AI context remains unresolved pending a CJEU reference on Art.22 and clinical AI."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#EHDS_Art23> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The cross-border infrastructure for primary use, to be distinguished from HealthData@EU at Art.75, which serves secondary use. Added in v5.1.0 because the two were previously conflatable: the superseded Art.50 entry described HealthData@EU using proposal numbering with no primary-use counterpart recorded."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#DataSubject> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "An individual whose personal data is processed" .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#NationalAuthority> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/mailo#LegalEntity> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#SoftwareAsMedicalDevice> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#PatientRight> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "PatientRight" .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#TSD_Art9> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Courts may impose confidentiality measures to protect trade secrets during litigation. Relevant to UPC discovery proceedings where a party seeks disclosure of AI model architecture or training data as part of infringement or validity analysis."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#Tension_EPO_MDR_SemanticScope> <https://w3id.org/mailo#involvesArticle> <https://w3id.org/mailo#MDR_AnnexII> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#riskLevel> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#AIAct_Art26> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Deployers, for example a hospital using a third-party system, must take appropriate technical and organisational measures to ensure use in accordance with the instructions (26(1)); assign human oversight to natural persons with the necessary competence, training and authority (26(2)); ensure input data is relevant and sufficiently representative for the intended purpose so far as they control it (26(4)); monitor operation and inform the provider of risks or serious incidents (26(5)); keep automatically generated logs for at least six months (26(6)); inform workers before putting a system into use in the workplace (26(7)); and, where a decision concerning a natural person is taken on the basis of an Annex III system's output, inform the affected person that they are subject to it (26(11)). Note that 26(11), like Art.86, is scoped to Annex III."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#RegulatoryAuthority> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "An EU or national regulatory or supervisory body" .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#Case> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Case"@en .
