<https://w3id.org/mailo#lit_Robaldo_L_Bartolini_C_Palmirani_M_et_al_2020_Fo> <https://w3id.org/mailo#relevanceScore> "5"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#lit_Frontiers_Digital_Health_2025_Ontologies_as_the_semantic> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "OWL2 + SWRL semantic reasoning enables auditable inference consistent with domain expertise. Ontologies encode explicit rules and traceable data relationships required by AI Act. Doctrinal scaffold for MAILO." .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#hasHighRiskRoute> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <https://w3id.org/mailo#AutomatedDecisionSystem> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#DataAct_Art23> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Medical AI providers using cloud infrastructure must enable switching between cloud providers without data lock-in. Relevant for hospital AI SaaS deployment models."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#EPO_T1191_19> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "EPO T 1191/19 — Neuronal plasticity/INSTITUT GUTTMANN: meta-learning, inventive step and training-data sufficiency"@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#EHDS_Art69> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Where a health data user seeks only an answer in anonymised statistical form, a health data request may be made instead of a data permit; the HDAB supplies the result rather than access to the underlying data. The least intrusive route in the access hierarchy and a candidate safeguard where a protected element under Art.52 cannot be adequately mitigated within a secure processing environment. Added in v5.1.0."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#lit_Andrade_F_Rebelo_D_M_2024_Schufa_s_Case_C-634_21_on> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/date> "2024" .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#triggersRoute> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Links a classification rule to the AI Act high-risk route it produces. MDR Annex VIII Rule 11 places most diagnostic and decision-support software at Class IIa or above, requiring notified-body assessment, which satisfies AI Act Art.6(1) and puts the system on the Annex I route — with the consequence that the Art.86 right to explanation does not apply. Encoded in v5.1.0 so the chain is queryable rather than only described in prose."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Ontology> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#Explanation> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#proposedEffect> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "proposed effect"@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#GDPR_Art9> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Art.9(1) PROHIBITS processing of special categories of personal data, including data concerning health. Art.9(2) sets out the exhaustive exceptions, of which (a) explicit consent and (g) substantial public interest are the two that can also unlock Art.22(4). Scope clarified in v5.1.0: the earlier comment described the article neutrally without recording that Art.9(1) is a prohibition."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#DataAct> <https://w3id.org/mailo#coversArticle> <https://w3id.org/mailo#DataAct_Art5> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#LegislativeAmendment> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#decidedBy> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#P_817_002> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Non-transparent, self-learning ML risk assessment systems may not be used for decisions with significant impact. Pre-determined criteria and regular review are required."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#C_307_22> <https://w3id.org/mailo#establishesPrinciple> <https://w3id.org/mailo#P_307_001> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#Tension_EPO_GDPR_Disclosure> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Re-anchored in v5.1.0 to TSD Art.1(2)(b), which preserves rules requiring disclosure of trade secrets to administrative or judicial authorities. The Art.5 exceptions previously relied on concern freedom of expression and whistleblowing, not data-subject or regulatory disclosure."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#lit_Frontiers_Digital_Health_2025_Ontologies_as_the_semantic> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/digital-health/articles/10.3389/fdgth.2025.1668385/full> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#GDPR_Art15_1h> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The data subject has the right to be informed of THE EXISTENCE of automated decision-making, including profiling, REFERRED TO IN ART.22(1) AND (4) and, at least in those cases, meaningful information about the logic involved as well as the significance and the envisaged consequences of such processing. Interpreted in C-203/22 paras 59-62. CORRECTED in v5.1.0: the earlier text omitted both the existence limb and the cross-reference scoping this right to Art.22(1) and (4) cases, which is what ties the explanation duty to the automated-decision regime rather than to processing at large."@en .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#EHDS> <https://w3id.org/mailo#coversArticle> <https://w3id.org/mailo#EHDS_Art52> .
<https://w3id.org/mailo#UPC_Sanofi_Amgen> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Inclusion basis (DirectSubjectMatter): Antibody claims; biologic rather than AI, retained for claim-scope reasoning."@en .
